<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Argive Heraion</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Heraion of Argos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Argive Heraion was the chief sanctuary of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, on a hillside between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>. Controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argos">Argos</a>, it had temples from the 7th century BCE, and after a fire in 423 BCE a new temple was built with a gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> statue of Hera by the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polykleitos">Polykleitos</a>. It is the setting of the story of Cleobis and Biton told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argive-heraion.jpg" alt="Argive Heraion" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Lippincott Tilton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraion_of_Argos,_Reconstruction_on_a_1902_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
